# Force LF for ES-module scripts regardless of core.autocrlf.
# vitest/vite-node mishandles a shebang line that ends in CRLF, so a Windows
# checkout (autocrlf=true) broke collection of e2e/scripts/*.test.mjs (#277).
# All tracked .mjs files were already LF in the index; this pins the working
# tree to match. Node, editors, and CI are all LF-safe.
*.mjs text eol=lf

# Source and config files are TEXT, and git must DIFF them as text even if one
# ever acquires a stray control byte.
#
# `text` alone is not enough, and that is the whole point of these lines: it
# governs EOL normalization only, so git still auto-detects "binary" from a NUL
# in the first 8000 bytes and prints `Binary files ... differ`. Setting the
# `diff` attribute to true is what forces a textual diff. Verified empirically:
# on a .cs file carrying one NUL, `*.cs text` and `*.cs text diff=csharp` BOTH
# still rendered as `Bin N -> N bytes`; only `*.cs text diff` rendered real
# +/- lines. (A named driver like diff=csharp selects funcname patterns but
# leaves binary detection to the auto-heuristic, so it does not help here.)
#
# Why it matters: a NUL typed into ExtractionPromptVersionTests.cs made git
# classify that file as binary, so the whole-wire-prompt SHA tripwire landed
# INVISIBLE in `git diff`, in `gh pr diff`, and in GitHub's PR view — on a
# branch whose merge depends on two reviewers reading exactly that diff. It
# also broke text 3-way merges of the one file that must be re-pinned on every
# prompt edit, and made `rg`/`grep` answer "Binary file matches" (#384).
#
# No `eol=` here on purpose: the index is already LF for every one of these and
# the working tree keeps following core.autocrlf, so this adds no checkout churn.
*.cs      text diff
*.csproj  text diff
*.slnx    text diff
*.ts      text diff
*.tsx     text diff
*.js      text diff
*.jsx     text diff
*.json    text diff
*.md      text diff
*.yml     text diff
*.yaml    text diff
*.sql     text diff
*.css     text diff
*.html    text diff
